#: src/functions.md:416
msgid ""
"Note that the escape sequence returned by `style(styles)` are prefixes. You "
"can use the `NORMAL` constant to reset the style after use:"
msgstr ""

#: src/functions.md:419
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"error message:\n"
"  echo '{{style(\"error\") + message + NORMAL}}'\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/functions.md:424
msgid ""
"`style(styles, text)`<sup>1.55.0</sup> Style `text` with `styles` as in the "
"one-argument form. The style is reset automatically, so use of `NORMAL` to "
"reset the terminal is not needed:"
msgstr ""

#: src/functions.md:428
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"error message:\n"
"  echo '{{style(\"error\", message)}}'\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/functions.md:433
msgid "User Directories"
msgstr ""

#: src/functions.md:435
msgid ""
"These functions<sup>1.23.0</sup> return paths to user-specific directories "
"for things like configuration, data, caches, executables, and the user’s "
"home directory."
msgstr ""

#: src/functions.md:439
msgid ""
"On Unix, these functions follow the [XDG Base Directory Specification]"
"(https://specifications.freedesktop.org/basedir-spec/basedir-spec-latest."
"html)."
msgstr ""

#: src/functions.md:442
msgid ""
"On macOS and Windows, these functions return the system-specified user-"
"specific directories. For example, `cache_directory()` returns `~/Library/"
"Caches` on macOS and `{FOLDERID_LocalAppData}` on Windows."
msgstr ""

#: src/functions.md:446
msgid ""
"See the [`dirs`](https://docs.rs/dirs/latest/dirs/index.html) crate for more "
"details."
msgstr ""

#: src/functions.md:449
msgid "`cache_directory()` - The user-specific cache directory."
msgstr ""

#: src/functions.md:450
msgid "`config_directory()` - The user-specific configuration directory."
msgstr ""

#: src/functions.md:451
msgid ""
"`config_local_directory()` - The local user-specific configuration directory."
